# Program vs Process
_____________________
=> A Program is a passive collection of instructions stored on disk, whereas a Process is an active instance of a program that is currently executing in memory.
   This is the foundation for everything that comes later:

Process States
Scheduling
Threads
Synchronization
Deadlocks

If Program vs Process is unclear, the rest of OS becomes difficult.

First Question

When you install Chrome, what actually gets installed?

Answer:

A file gets stored in SSD.

Example:

chrome.exe

This file is called a Program.

What is a Program?
Definition

A Program is a set of instructions stored on secondary storage (SSD/HDD).

Easy Definition

A Program is a file containing instructions that tell the computer what to do.

Examples

Stored on SSD:

chrome.exe
spotify.exe
notepad.exe
vscode.exe

All of these are programs.

Important Point

A program is passive.

Meaning:

It is doing nothing.

Example

Suppose Chrome is installed.

But you haven't opened it.

chrome.exe

is simply sitting inside SSD.

No CPU usage.

No RAM usage.

No execution.

This is just a Program.

Real-Life Example

Think of a recipe book.

The recipe exists.

But nobody is cooking.

So food is not being prepared.

Recipe Book = Program

What is a Process?

Now you double-click Chrome.

Immediately:

OS loads Chrome into RAM.

CPU starts executing its instructions.

Now Chrome becomes:

Process
Definition

A Process is a program that is currently being executed.

Easy Definition

A Process is a running instance of a program.

Real-Life Example

Recipe Book = Program

Cooking Food = Process

Another Example:

Movie file stored in SSD = Program

Movie currently playing = Process

Program vs Process Visualization

Before opening Chrome:

SSD
 └── chrome.exe

Program

After opening Chrome:

SSD
 └── chrome.exe

RAM
 └── Chrome Running

Process

What Happens Internally?

You double-click Chrome.

OS performs:

Step 1

Finds:

chrome.exe
Step 2

Loads code into RAM.

Step 3

Allocates memory.

Step 4

Creates Process Control Block (PCB)

Step 5

Assigns CPU time.

Step 6

Chrome starts running.

Now it is a Process.

Can One Program Create Multiple Processes?

Absolutely.

Example:

Open Chrome.

Open another Chrome window.

Open another Chrome profile.

OS may create multiple processes.

Example:

chrome.exe

One Program

Chrome Process 1
Chrome Process 2
Chrome Process 3
-------------------------------------
| Program           | Process       |
| ----------------- | ------------- |
| Stored in SSD/HDD | Stored in RAM |
| Passive           | Active        |
| Not executing     | Executing     |
| Static            | Dynamic       |
| No CPU usage      | Uses CPU      |
| No state          | Has state     |
|-----------------------------------|

